The red line shows the theoretical ideal distribution of normal distribution of each group. The visual fit of the data to a standard bell shape may be evaluated by comparing the actual data (blue bars) with this curve. This will enable us to identify skewness or aberrations before attempting to conduct the formal statistical tests.

3.3	Useful information for the data understanding
The standard bell-shaped characteristic of the normal distribution is used in the Normal group. The group of the pathological is multimodal (more than one peak), probably because of the reduced sample size (N=176). But, at least in terms of visual indicators, both distributions are concentrated around the same value (average is around 132 bpm), indicating that the means differ by no apparent amount between the groups. 
4	Analysis
4.1	Statistical test used to test the hypotheses and output 
An independent samples t-test was chosen where we compared the heart rates of the two different groups. As this is a continuous dependent variable, this parametric test was the most logical. Although the Shapiro-Wilk test raised the non-normality alarm (which is common with large samples (N=1655)) our visual examination of the histograms revealed that the data was bell-shaped enough to continue. Moreover, the test of equal variances performed by Levene was found to have no differences between the variances of the data (p >.05), which proved compliance with the requirements predetermined by the validity of the analysis.
4.2	The null hypothesis is rejected /not rejected based on the p-value 
It was found that there was no significant difference in baseline heart rate between the groups ( t (214) = 0.39, p =.694). Since the p-value exceeds the 0.05 level significantly, we do not reject the null hypothesis. This means that heart rate is not a good predictor of fetal pathology. To be sure that these outcomes were not a distribution anomaly, we did a repeat Mann-Whitney U test, which validated this non-significant result again (p =.502).
 
6	Conclusions
6.1	Results explained
We statistically calculated that the mean heart rate of healthy fetuses was very close to that of pathological fetuses in terms of mean ( M = 131.98bpm) and standard deviation ( SD = 0.528). The p-value obtained on the independent t-test was 0.694 that is well above the usual significance cutoff. Consequently, we do not reject the null hypothesis. This proves that in this data there is no significant difference in baseline heart rate depending on fetal health status..
6.2	Interpretation of the results 
These findings have implications on our Research Question by suggesting that baseline heart rate is an inappropriate predictor of fetal health on its own. To the general medical community, it indicates that a normal rate (110160 bpm) is not the only reassuring element. Instead of diagnostic emphasis should be on dynamic patterns, including variability and decelerations, since a troubled unborn baby may frequently be able to hold a steady baseline to the final stages of decompensation.
6.3	Reasons and/or implications for future work, limitations of your study
The major weakness of our study was that the imbalance between the two sets of data was huge, as the number of pathological cases (N=176) was significantly outnumbered by the number of normal ones (N=1655). The research on the subject should not be confined to examining individual variables. Rather than that, it would be far more accurate to use multivariate analysis to analyse the interaction of baseline heart rate with uterine contractions to give a more comprehensive view of complex physiology of fetal distress.

